{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And the man shall bring his wife, etc.</b> Until the end of the entire matter. There is a fine point to raise about the subject: (1) What is the intention of the phrase <b>a meal offering of remembrance, a reminder of iniquity</b> — why was remembrance necessary, all the more so since the event is not far removed to require remembrance? Also, why should it be attributed specifically to a barley meal offering that it is a reminder of iniquity — for I see that it is a new meal-offering for Israel from the day after Passover. Further, one must understand the intention in this act of the holy waters: what is the waters' mode of action, and why are they called holy? Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 15a, Sifrei) that the text intends to say that they were sanctified in a vessel, namely the Basin [Kior]; yet even regarding this one must seek understanding as to why HaShem would command such a thing. Also, what is the effect of the scroll in making her drink from it? Granted, what Moses did with the calf, when he made Israel drink, was along the lines of ״let your own evil chastise you״ (Jeremiah 2:19). Also, why from the Sanctuary? Also, the precision that it be from what is prepared in the Sanctuary (Sotah ibid.) and that he not dig [for earth] — the matter is wondrous to me. Also, the verse says <b>and he shall put into the water</b>, and they of blessed memory said (ibid.) that the earth must float upon the surface of the water, and the Rabbis hold that if he put the earth before the water it is invalid — what prevents this? And it appears to me that the verse did not mention in this commandment a statute to seal the mouth of the questioner, as if to say: you shall not ask. Also, it says <b>the bitter waters</b> — I do not know why they should be called bitter. Our Rabbis of blessed memory in the Sifrei said: ״bitter״ — the waters are transformed to become bitter. Another interpretation: they are called so on account of their end-result, for they embitter the body. And in the Gemara (Sotah 20a) Samuel's father said that one must put something bitter into the waters. What is the reason? As it is written: ״the bitter [ha-marim]״ — meaning they are already bitter, end of discussion. And these matters are astonishing: why did HaShem seal these matters in this commandment, in addition to the wonderment at the matter in its entirety." "<b>And it is possible</b> to explain the matter according to what has preceded for us in the inner knowledge of the Torah. Have I not made it known to you in the pericope of Genesis (Genesis 2:1) at the verse ״Thus the heavens were finished״ etc., ״and Elohim finished״ etc. — that every created and existing being longs and consummates its desire to cleave to Elohim, the living God, blessed be He. And this felt yearning exists also in inanimate objects, each according to the measure of spirituality that the Creator has imprinted in it, commensurate with what is needed for its existence and to render praise to its Maker, as it says (Proverbs 16:4): ״YHWH has made all things for His own purpose.״ Further, we find in the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory who said (Genesis Rabbah ch. 5) a reason why they are called weeping waters — because HaShem split the waters: half above and half remained below, beneath the dry land. The waters that remained below weep and sigh over the fact that they did not merit to draw near to the living God in the upper half; for this is the longing of all created beings together, and their hope." "<b>Further</b>, He showed us, HaShem, that which He desired — to establish His dwelling among the lower realms from the days of man. But through his sin the earth was cursed, and the throne of sovereignty, the wellspring of blessing, was raised away from the world (Genesis Rabbah ch. 19), until the time His word arrived on the day of the giving of the Torah, and He desired the work of His hands to dwell among the lower ones. And this is your sign: His saying in the wilderness to the children of Israel to make for Him a Sanctuary. And He did not wait until they came to an inhabited land, for there would have remained but a short time, had it not been for the episode of the spies. And when iniquity darkened, even after the Shekhinah had descended and His glory had dwelt for many years, HaShem withdrew His Shekhinah and returned to His original place — the heavens on high. And from now on, absent the sins that surround it, the dwelling of the living God among the lower ones would persist; and through this HaShem would wipe away the tear of the weeping waters and remove the disgrace of the earth, and the waters would rejoice and the earth would exult — she whom HaShem had cursed, and from whom the eternal Beloved had been distant. And behold, now is the time of the Beloved. But if sins surround it, the matter would revert to what it had been, and the throne would be raised aloft again; and over this the beloved ones would mourn at the voice of the gushing waters. And one must attune the ear to these teachings, for the improvement of Israel when they stood at Mount Sinai, and their fitness, was also worthy of settling His Shekhinah, blessed be He, upon the earth — and ״the earth shall be full of the knowledge of HaShem״ — and the dwelling of HaShem among the lower ones as among the upper ones, and our place would be elevated. For the ascent and the descent depend on their nearness before HaShem." "<b>And you will find</b> that they of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 41:7): ״freedom from the angel of death,״ etc., freedom from the evil inclination. But through the sin of the calf the impairment returned to what it had been, and the Satan thrust out his head, and the improvement of Israel availed only for a confined space within their camp — but the world at large was rendered speechless [נשמ\"ו] on a day of wrath, and the measure of death returned to the world. Unlike the time that is hoped for, when every aspect of evil will be annihilated, as it says (Zechariah 13:2): ״and I will remove the spirit of impurity from the land״ — then our land will be called the land of the living, for death is swallowed up forever." "<b>And keep</b> these matters, and by means of them we shall ascend on the proper path of the matter's intention. For HaShem commands, in the jealousy of a man for his wife, that He test her through this act: he shall bring her to the place where the divine presence camps [חונה האלהים]. And I have already alerted you that that place is called ״the upper land״ because the Shekhinah dwelt there, and it is not included in that which HaShem cursed. Also, the waters that are there — their eyes shed no tear, for why should they weep, and why should their heart be grieved? And HaShem commanded that he take from those waters, which are called holy — they are called holy by virtue of their nearness before HaShem, holy and awesome is His name. And through this He has signaled to you the mode of action of the waters. And my words do not contradict, heaven forfend, the words of our Rabbis who said they were sanctified in the Basin — rather, from the fact that HaShem spoke in this manner, it intends, according to the living God, yet an additional meaning." "He also commanded to take dust from the Sanctuary specifically, because it is close to the Holy One, blessed is He, on high, and He commanded that one not dig with a utensil but rather [take] from the dust that is prepared before HaShem without any interruption of construction, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 15a), because they are closer and feel the indwelling of the Shekhinah upon them — something that this [dust] outside the Sanctuary does not feel, for it has never tasted this quality until now, to do what HaShem wishes to be done." "<b>And He commanded</b> HaShem that the water be placed first and the dust above. The reason is according to the order in which they exist in the world. And you will find that the Sages who said that if he put the dust first he has done nothing — they said that if they were mixed together it is valid, and it is the very same idea that they are arranged as water and dust, for there is a portion where the water is below and the dust above, and there is a portion where the water is next to the dust; but dust below — this pattern does not exist in the order of living water. And according to R. Shimon, who said there (Sotah 16a) that even dust below is valid, he holds that one need not be particular, for there is water above the dust, and it suffices that the water be sanctified in the vessel, and this is their vitality to feel the sensation required for the matter." "<b>And He commanded</b> HaShem to write the passage of the sotah with the divine Names therein, and to erase all the writing in that water and dust, and the reason is to invest them with power to perform the effect that they are to accomplish." "And He further commanded that she bring a meal offering of barley. The reason is that it parallels the inferior offering that Cain brought at the beginning of the world, and this was occasioned by the sin of Adam and Eve, and it is a remembrance of the transgression that caused weeping to the waters and curse to the earth, and this is the meaning of <b>a meal offering of remembrance, bringing iniquity to remembrance</b>. And then he makes the woman drink the waters. Now, when those waters enter the woman's innards with the Name united within them, if the woman is impure — behold, that Name, by means of the meal offering that brings iniquity to remembrance, will recall the former transgression, and the waters will take effect. And thereby they become bitter, for they recall their grief and their weeping, and HaShem wipes away their tears. And behold, the impure woman causes their bitterness to revolve, and the bitter waters that entered her become increasingly bitter — they cut her innards and take vengeance upon her, for this is the woman who caused their weeping, and with the power of the Name placed in them, they exact their vengeance from her. And one who knows the sin of Eve through the defilement of the serpent knows that this is the primordial transgression that caused the Shekhinah of the supernal Light to ascend from this lower world, and accordingly — shall a man find his enemy and send him away? — they fulfill in her: 'If one arises to kill you, kill him first,' and they take vengeance upon her, both the waters and the dust, for both of them have reason for harm. And even though Scripture mentioned only waters, the dust too is included."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :register {:flags {1 ["God"], 2 ["God"], 4 ["God"]}}, :notes {2 "The verse cited in paraphrase, 'the earth shall be full of the knowledge of HaShem,' alludes to Isaiah 11:9.", 3 "נשמ\"ו (rendered 'rendered speechless') reads as an acronym or possibly a textual corruption; translated hyper-literally per the translator's protocol."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:55.335620698Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}